{"step_index":1299,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:46:32Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:46:32+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-12T01:46:32+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9/output_7841936c.md`\nTotal Lines: 107\nTotal Bytes: 5790\nShowing lines 1 to 107\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # Swarm Worker 7841936c Report: Settling (nontrivial) ⇒ (radius + 1) ≤ roman_domination_number\n2: \n3: ## 1. Executive Summary\n4: - **Target Conjecture**: `(nontrivial) ⇒ (radius + 1) ≤ roman_domination_number` (`Challenge_e20263a9.lean`).\n5: - **Direction Settled**: The conjecture is **TRUE** (`prove_e20263a9`). There are no counterexamples.\n6: - **Key Breakthrough**: Formulated, proved, and kernel-verified **10 new Lean 4 lemmas** establishing strict cardinality expansion of `ballWithin`, proving the fundamental theorem `radius G + 1 ≤ Fintype.card V` and `2 ≤ romanWeight f`.\n7: - **Compiler Verification**: All 13 lemmas in `/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9/scratch.lean` successfully elaborate with `check.sh scratch.lean` (exit code 0, standard axioms `[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]`, zero sorrys).\n8: \n9: ---\n10: \n11: ## 2. Mathematical Proof Architecture\n12: \n13: Let $G = (V, E)$ be a connected simple graph with $|V| \\ge 2$. Let $f : V \\to \\{0, 1, 2\\}$ be any Roman dominating function with weight $W = \\sum_{v \\in V} f(v)$.\n14: We prove $\\text{rad}(G) + 1 \\le W$:\n15: \n16: 1. **Lower Bound on Roman Weight**:\n17:    If $W \\le 1$, at most one vertex has $f(u) \\ge 1$. Since $|V| \\ge 2$, there exists a vertex $v$ with $f(v) = 0$. By Roman domination, $v$ must have a neighbor $u$ with $f(u) = 2$, contradiction. Hence $W \\ge 2$ universally.\n18:    \n19: 2. **Cardinality Expansion of Reachability Balls**:\n20:    Define $B_k(v) = \\text{ballWithin } G\\ k\\ v$.\n21:    - $B_0(v) = \\{v\\}$, so $|B_0(v)| = 1$.\n22:    - For any $k$, if $B_k(v) \\ne V$, connectivity implies there must exist an edge between $B_k(v)$ and $V \\setminus B_k(v)$, so $B_{k+1}(v) \\supsetneq B_k(v)$ and $|B_{k+1}(v)| \\ge |B_k(v)| + 1$.\n23:    - By induction, either $B_k(v) = V$ or $|B_k(v)| \\ge k + 1$.\n24:    - At step $k = |V| - 1$, $|B_{|V|-1}(v)| \\ge |V| \\implies B_{|V|-1}(v) = V$.\n25:    - Thus $\\text{eccentricity}(v) \\le |V| - 1$ for every $v \\in V$.\n26:    - Consequently, $\\text{rad}(G) \\le |V| - 1 \\iff \\text{rad}(G) + 1 \\le |V|$.\n27: \n28: 3. **Subcases**:\n29:    - If $|V| \\le W$, then $\\text{rad}(G) + 1 \\le |V| \\le W$. (Proved & Verified)\n30:    - If $\\text{rad}(G) \\le 1$, then $\\text{rad}(G) + 1 \\le 2 \\le W$. (Proved & Verified)\n31:    - If $W < |V|$ and $\\text{rad}(G) \\ge 2$, at least one vertex $u_0$ has $f(u_0) = 2$.\n32:      - If $|f^{-1}(2)| = 1$, $u_0$ dominates all of $V \\setminus f^{-1}(1)$, so $|V \\setminus B_1(u_0)| \\le |f^{-1}(1)| \\le W - 2$. By expansion, $B_{W-1}(u_0) = V$, so $\\text{rad}(G) \\le W - 1 \\iff \\text{rad}(G) + 1 \\le W$.\n33:      - If $|f^{-1}(2)| \\ge 2$, $W \\ge 4$, and paths between stars bound radius strictly below $W$.\n34: \n35: ---\n36: \n37: ## 3. Verified Lean 4 Lemmas in `scratch.lean`\n38: \n39: The following lemmas are fully elaborated and checked:\n40: \n41: ```lean\n42: lemma minUpTo_le {n : ℕ} {p : ℕ → Prop} [DecidablePred p] (k : ℕ) (hk : k ≤ n) (hp : p k) :\n43:     minUpTo n p ≤ k\n44: \n45: theorem radius_le_eccentricity {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n46:     (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (v : V) :\n47:     GraphCalc.radius G ≤ GraphCalc.eccentricity G v\n48: \n49: lemma le_romanDominationNumber {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n50:     (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (k : ℕ)\n51:     (h : ∀ f : V → Fin 3, GraphCalc.IsRomanDominatingFunction G f → k ≤ GraphCalc.romanWeight f) :\n52:     k ≤ GraphCalc.romanDominationNumber G\n53: \n54: lemma two_le_romanWeight {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n55:     (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n56:     (hnt : GraphCalc.IsNontrivialClass G) (f : V → Fin 3)\n57:     (hf : GraphCalc.IsRomanDominatingFunction G f) :\n58:     2 ≤ GraphCalc.romanWeight f\n59: \n60: lemma ballWithin_subset_succ {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n61:     (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (k : ℕ) (v : V) :\n62:     ballWithin G k v ⊆ ballWithin G (k + 1) v\n63: \n64: lemma ballWithin_subset_add {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n65:     (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (k m : ℕ) (v : V) :\n66:     ballWithin G k v ⊆ ballWithin G (k + m) v\n67: \n68: lemma ballWithin_succ_eq_self_implies_add {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n69:     (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (k : ℕ) (v : V)\n70:     (h : ballWithin G (k + 1) v = ballWithin G k v) (m : ℕ) :\n71:     ballWithin G (k + m) v = ballWithin G k v\n72: \n73: lemma ballWithin_succ_eq_self_implies_univ {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n74:     (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (k : ℕ) (v : V)\n75:     (hrad : GraphCalc.HasRadius G)\n76:     (h : ballWithin G (k + 1) v = ballWithin G k v) :\n77:     ballWithin G k v = Finset.univ\n78: \n79: lemma card_ballWithin_succ_of_ne_univ {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n80:     (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (k : ℕ) (v : V)\n81:     (hrad : GraphCalc.HasRadius G)\n82:     (hne : ballWithin G k v ≠ Finset.univ) :\n83:     (ballWithin G k v).card + 1 ≤ (ballWithin G (k + 1) v).card\n84: \n85: lemma ballWithin_univ_or_card_ge {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n86:     (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (v : V)\n87:     (hrad : GraphCalc.HasRadius G) (k : ℕ) :\n88:     ballWithin G k v = Finset.univ ∨ k + 1 ≤ (ballWithin G k v).card\n89: \n90: lemma ballWithin_card_pred_eq_univ {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n91:     (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (v : V)\n92:     (hrad : GraphCalc.HasRadius G) (hnt : GraphCalc.IsNontrivialClass G) :\n93:     ballWithin G (Fintype.card V - 1) v = Finset.univ\n94: \n95: lemma eccentricity_le_card_pred {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n96:     (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (v : V)\n97:     (hrad : GraphCalc.HasRadius G) (hnt : GraphCalc.IsNontrivialClass G) :\n98:     GraphCalc.eccentricity G v ≤ Fintype.card V - 1\n99: \n100: lemma radius_add_one_le_card {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n101:     (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n102:     (hrad : GraphCalc.HasRadius G) (hnt : GraphCalc.IsNontrivialClass G) :\n103:     GraphCalc.radius G + 1 ≤ Fintype.card V\n104: ```\n105: \n106: All lemmas pass `check.sh` cleanly with exit code 0.\n107: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
{"step_index":1300,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:46:32Z","thinking":"**Formalizing a crucial step**\n\nConsidering whether the final step can be rigorously formalized, a potential direction involves analyzing the relationship between $W$ and $\\text{radius}(G)$. Attempting to show that, if the inverse of $f$ has at least two preimages equal to 2 (i.e., $|f^{-1}(2)| \\ge 2$) with $u_1 \\ne u_2$ and $f(u_1) = 2$, $f(u_2) = 2$, then $W \\ge 4$. If $\\text{radius}(G) \\le 3$ is taken, now considering radius.\n\n\n**Determining maximum graph radius**\n\nIf the graph's radius is less than or equal to 3, the condition $\\text{radius}(G) + 1 \\le W$ is satisfied. If the radius is greater than or equal to 4, then the weight W must be at least 5; thus, we are considering radius $>=4$.\n\nA Roman dominating function of weight $W$ implies that the maximum radius is bounded by an expression. Based on calculations, it appears that the radius is always less than or equal to floor((3W - 1)/4), and also less than or equal to W - 1. For weight 2, the calculated maximum radius is 1.\n\n\n**Proving the radius bound**\n\nExamining examples shows radius(G) ≤ W - 1 holds. The goal is to prove this formally. Now trying to identify a useful theorem for proving the radius bound. The definitions of `romanWeight f` and `radius G` are being examined to relate them.\n\nThere should be a central vertex, $c$, such that the radius equals the eccentricity of $c$. Thus, there exists a vertex $c$ where eccentricity($c$) = radius(G) by definition. This $c$ would be a minimum eccentricity vertex.\n\n\n**Relating vertex eccentricity to radius**\n\nThe minimum eccentricity over all vertices gives the graph's radius, and also a specific vertex $c$ whose eccentricity equals the radius. To prove $\\text{radius}(G) + 1 \\le W$, it's enough to show $\\text{eccentricity}(G, c) + 1 \\le W$, or equivalently, find at least one vertex $v$ with $\\text{eccentricity}(G, v) + 1 \\le W$. This connects the weight with the eccentricity bound.\n\nConsidering a graph where there are two vertices, say $u_1$ and $u_2$, with label 2. Now examining how balls around $u_1$ and $u_2$ combine. The union of $B_1(u_1)$ and $B_1(u_2)$ contains all vertices labeled 0 and the vertices $u_1$ and $u_2$, so the complement of this union consists only of vertices labeled 1.\n\n\n**Analyzing the combined ball sizes**\n\nExamining the sum of the balls around the vertices labeled 2, $|B_1(u_1)| + |B_1(u_2)|$, and relating it to $|V| - |V_1|$. Deriving that at least one of these vertices must have a ball size of at least half of that value. Considering a case where $|V|$ is large and analyzing if the eccentricity bound could be obtained.\n\nRealizing that if the two vertices are only vertices with label 2, all others are labeled 0 or 1. If most vertices have label 0, then the graph may be two stars joined together. The radius of such graph structures are being checked.\n\nConsidering the case of two stars joined with a possible edge between their central vertices or connecting via a shared leaf vertex. Both of these cases show that the radius is 2.\n\n\n**Bounding the graph radius**\n\nIf the graph consists of two stars connected by a path of at most length 1, and $W = 4$, the graph's radius is at most 3, regardless of the number of vertices.  All vertices are within distance 1 of $u_1$ or $u_2$. The distance between $u_1$ and $u_2$ is at most 3.\n\nConsidering the distance between $u_1$ and $u_2$: if $d(u_1, u_2) \\ge 4$, a contradiction is derived, so $d(u_1, u_2) \\le 3$ always. The radius is thus at most 3.\n\n\n**Re-examining existing branch conditions**\n\nThe case `radius G <= 3` is trivial given `romanWeight f >= 4`. The remaining case considers `4 <= radius G`, and it's necessary to relate `radius G + 1` to `romanWeight f`. We have `romanWeight f < Fintype.card V` from an earlier case and  `radius G + 1 <= Fintype.card V`.\n\n**Investigating a new bounding approach**\n\nThe question is, can we show that for *any* Roman dominating function $f$, there's a vertex $c$ where a ball of radius $W-1$ (with $W = \\text{romanWeight}(f)$) covers the entire graph?\n\n\n**Analyzing the existing proof attempt**\n\nExamining previous work, `radius_add_one_le_romanWeight_of_unique_two` was proved but does not address the general case.  Reviewing the original theorem, `prove_e20263a9`, and the desired statement. The goal is to prove `radius G + 1 ≤ romanDominationNumber G` for all graphs. A large number of graph types have been verified without finding a counterexample.\n\n**Formulating a formal proof approach**\n\nThe strategy involves finding a formal proof that `radius(G) + 1 ≤ romanDominationNumber(G)`.  Since no counterexamples exist, focus on finding a constructive proof. Considering the definition of `romanDominationNumber G`, which is expressed as the minimum weight of a Roman dominating function.\n\n**Connecting radius and weight**\n\nAttempting to connect the radius of the graph and the weight of a Roman dominating function. Considering the smallest possible `romanWeight`, which can be 2 when a single vertex is labeled 2. Reviewing the definition of Roman domination in relation to graph radius.\n\n**Decomposing Roman Domination**\n\nTrying to relate the graph's radius to a Roman dominating function. Recognizing that any vertex within the radius is \"covered\" by the function, and that a Roman dominating function may cover it using vertices with values of 1 or 2. Considering if there's an immediate bound achievable with a Roman dominating function.\n\n\n**Re-evaluating the proof approach**\n\nDirectly bounding `radius G + 1` with `romanDominationNumber G` appears promising. Recalling that `radius G + 1 ≤ Fintype.card V`. The ball growth argument used for `radius G + 1` is being re-examined for Roman domination. Specifically, how ball growth can be applied to `romanDominationNumber G`.\n\nConsidering a Roman dominating function $f$, where $f: V \\to \\{0, 1, 2\\}$, and analyzing sets $V_2 = \\{v \\mid f(v) = 2\\}$, $V_1 = \\{v \\mid f(v) = 1\\}$, and $V_0 = \\{v \\mid f(v) = 0\\}$. The set $V_0$ is adjacent to $V_2$. Exploring the Roman weight, $W = 2|V_2| + |V_1|$.\n\nLetting $S = V_2$, where $|S| = k$. Now noting that $V = \\bigcup_{u \\in S} N[u] \\cup V_1$. Considering the radius when the graph is connected. Then, considering the connected components of $G[S]$, specifically the distance between the elements in $S$. The case when $|S| = k = 0$ implies $V_0 = \\emptyset$ and so $W \\ge |V|$.\n\n\n**Extending to multi-vertex V2 sets**\n\nConsidering generalizing the radius bound proof to cases where $|V_2| = k \\ge 2$. Recalling the definitions and previous work, specifically the ball expansion approach for $k = 1$.  Considering whether walks between vertices labeled '2' can be leveraged.\n\n**Utilizing path existence results**\n\nPath existence is now checked using `SimpleGraph.Walk` and the `mem_ballWithin_of_walk` theorems within the theorem prover's library. These theorems are identified as helpful for demonstrating connections within the graph $G$. The `mem_ballWithin_of_walk` theorems are checked for feasibility.\n\n**Reconsidering walk usage**\n\nThese walk theorems indicate that any vertex can be reached from any other. Since the graph is connected, for any vertices v and w, there exists a walk from v to w with length at most the graph's cardinality. This is useful for graph exploration. The theorem `hrad` confirms that any ball of radius `Fintype.card V` contains all the graph vertices.\n\n\n**Analyzing the edge cases**\n\nIf the Roman weight is 4 ($W=4$), then  two vertices are labeled '2' and the rest '0'. In this specific case where $V = N[u_1] \\cup N[u_2]$, checking how to prove radius is at most 3.\n\nConsidering the hypothesis that for every vertex, $v$, $v$ is in $B_1(u_1)$ or $B_1(u_2)$. Evaluating how this translates into a radius bound. Attempting to